      "internal": [
        {
          "severity": "major",
          "file": "internal/brief/templates/run-retro.md",
          "line": 1,
          "title": "Retro instructions still say \"dispatch→commit timings\" after WaveTiming became dispatch→close",
          "detail": "Task 11 changes WaveTiming from one entry per committed attempt to one entry per dispatched attempt that *closed*, with `closed_at` always present and `committed`/`committed_at` only when the attempt actually committed (internal/finish/retro.go's WaveTiming doc comment now reads \"one per dispatched attempt that closed\"). The retro-writing op's own instructions, run-retro.md line 1, still describe the inputs as \"per-wave dispatch→commit timings\" — unchanged except for the review-findings clause the task explicitly edited. A reworked attempt's timing entry has no `committed_at` at all, which the stale phrasing does not prepare the retro-writing agent for.",
          "lenses": [
            "docs"
          ]
        },
        {
          "severity": "minor",
          "file": "internal/cli/cmd_close_wave.go",
          "line": 163,
          "title": "reviewFindingsOf's summation is only ever exercised with one reviewed task",
          "detail": "reviewFindingsOf sums len(tr.Review.Findings) across every task in the close. The only test that reaches it, TestWaveClosedEventCarriesSliceAndReviewFindings (internal/cli/close_events_test.go), bumps a single task (task 3) with one finding, so the accumulation (n += ...) across two-or-more reviewed tasks in the same close is never proved. TestRetroInputsCountEveryReviewOnce (internal/finish/retro_test.go) tests the downstream retro math but constructs its wave_closed events with a hard-coded review_findings value rather than calling reviewFindingsOf, so it doesn't cover this function either. A silent regression here (e.g. assignment instead of accumulation) would produce quietly wrong review_findings counts in the retro with no failing test.",
          "lenses": [
            "tests"
          ]
        },
        {
          "severity": "minor",
          "file": "internal/finish/retro.go",
          "line": 118,
          "title": "retro.go re-declares gate.EvReviewed under a different local name",
          "detail": "evGateReviewed = \"gate_reviewed\" duplicates the exported gate.EvReviewed (internal/gate/gate.go:47, also \"gate_reviewed\") even though retro.go already imports internal/gate (used elsewhere in the file, e.g. gate.FollowUp, gate.SourceApprove). Task 10's cmd_review.go uses gate.EvReviewed directly for the same event type; task 11 instead re-declares the same string under a new unexported name rather than reusing the existing constant it could import.",
          "lenses": [
            "consistency"
          ]
        }
      ]
